# AGY Implementation Plan: Integrating Cheat-Engine-Style Memory Scanner into Memory Viewer

## Executive Summary
This plan outlines the architecture, component design, and phased integration strategy to incorporate the Cheat-Engine-style Frida Memory Scanner specified in `plan.md` into the existing `memory_viewer` project. 

The existing codebase consists of a PySide6 GUI (`ui/main_window.py`, `ui/hex_view.py`) and a basic Frida backend (`frida_backend/reader.py`, `frida_backend/agent.js`). The integration will transform the project into a comprehensive memory analysis tool, featuring both a standalone interactive CLI (`memscan`) and a rich GUI Scanner tab, powered by a modular high-performance Frida JavaScript engine.

---

## Key Architectural Principles & Requirements
1. **Zero Mass Memory Transfer**: All memory scanning, candidate filtering, and comparisons must occur natively inside the target process via the Frida JS agent. Python will only receive match address batches and summary statistics.
2. **64-bit Precision Safety**: 64-bit integers (`int64`/`uint64`) and native pointers must be handled using Frida's `Int64`/`UInt64`/`NativePointer` abstractions and string representations to prevent JavaScript `Number` precision degradation.
3. **Modular JS Agent**: The Frida agent will be modularized into discrete JS files (`agent.js`, `ranges.js`, `types.js`, `scanner.js`, `memory.js`) and concatenated/bundled at runtime for injection.
4. **Dual Interface**: A full interactive CLI (`memscan`) will be provided alongside an upgraded PySide6 GUI interface that exposes memory scanning controls, candidate lists, and hex dumping.
5. **Non-Blocking Execution & Cancellation**: Long scans will run asynchronously with periodic JS cancellation checks and progress updates back to Python via Frida messaging.

---

